1
00:00:00,003 --> 00:00:01,213
ApnaSOR.

2
00:00:01,253 --> 00:00:03,842
A smart order router for crypto exchanges,

3
00:00:03,882 --> 00:00:08,630
built by group CS14 at Usman
Institute of Technology.

4
00:00:10,166 --> 00:00:11,183
Who is it for?

5
00:00:11,223 --> 00:00:14,727
People who place crypto orders
and care about the price they get:

6
00:00:14,767 --> 00:00:19,456
a trader working an order, the risk manager
who must be sure nothing goes wrong,

7
00:00:19,496 --> 00:00:22,111
and the administrator who runs the desk.

8
00:00:22,151 --> 00:00:25,681
ApnaSOR is the order
router of Apna Exchange:

9
00:00:25,721 --> 00:00:29,289
its users' orders go to the
best real exchange behind it.

10
00:00:29,329 --> 00:00:35,230
The problem: the same coin trades on
Binance, Bybit, OKX and HTX at once,

11
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:38,941
each with its own price, its
own fee and its own order book,

12
00:00:38,981 --> 00:00:41,928
and a person with four
tabs open is guessing.

13
00:00:42,878 --> 00:00:43,882
An example.

14
00:00:43,922 --> 00:00:45,970
A trader wants to buy one Bitcoin.

15
00:00:46,010 --> 00:00:51,021
Binance asks eighty thousand seven
hundred dollars, Bybit six hundred ninety,

16
00:00:51,061 --> 00:00:55,686
OKX six hundred eighty-five,
Gate six hundred ninety-five.

17
00:00:55,726 --> 00:00:57,220
OKX looks cheapest.

18
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:02,439
But each exchange charges a taker fee: a
tenth of a percent on Binance and Bybit,

19
00:01:02,479 --> 00:01:04,939
more on OKX, double on Gate.

20
00:01:04,979 --> 00:01:09,707
After fees, Bybit is cheapest, and
the one that looked cheapest is third,

21
00:01:09,747 --> 00:01:12,696
about thirty-five dollars
apart on one Bitcoin.

22
00:01:12,736 --> 00:01:17,877
ApnaSOR does this on every order,
on live books, in milliseconds,

23
00:01:17,917 --> 00:01:22,464
and takes the rest from the next cheapest
level anywhere when one exchange runs

24
00:01:22,504 --> 00:01:23,186
thin.

25
00:01:23,226 --> 00:01:25,055
And before anything is sent,

26
00:01:25,095 --> 00:01:28,663
every order passes seven risk
checks and a funding check,

27
00:01:28,703 --> 00:01:32,089
so a fat finger never reaches an exchange.

28
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:36,027
In one system: live order
books from the real exchanges;

29
00:01:36,067 --> 00:01:39,893
the best price after fees, with
a proof for every decision;

30
00:01:39,933 --> 00:01:42,599
seven risk checks and a kill switch;

31
00:01:42,639 --> 00:01:45,602
algorithms that work
large orders over time;

32
00:01:45,642 --> 00:01:49,532
research and a copilot that
proposes while a person confirms;

33
00:01:49,572 --> 00:01:52,367
a wallet reconciled against the exchanges;

34
00:01:52,407 --> 00:01:54,184
an audit log of everything.

35
00:01:54,224 --> 00:01:58,334
All of it runs against the exchanges'
own test systems with play money,

36
00:01:58,374 --> 00:02:02,483
and the same code runs on a
real-money server behind extra rails.

37
00:02:02,523 --> 00:02:03,757
Let's see it.

38
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:05,517
This is the trader's screen.

39
00:02:05,557 --> 00:02:06,509
Log in;

40
00:02:06,549 --> 00:02:09,254
every login is written to the audit log.

41
00:02:09,294 --> 00:02:11,921
The Dashboard: four exchanges connected,

42
00:02:11,961 --> 00:02:16,006
a local copy of each order book
kept in sync from its live feed,

43
00:02:16,046 --> 00:02:20,736
and on the right the best bid and the best
ask across all of them for twenty coins,

44
00:02:20,776 --> 00:02:23,480
with the exchange that holds each price.

45
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:26,713
On the left, every order
the router has sent.

46
00:02:28,459 --> 00:02:32,478
A real order, in the sandbox:
buy a small amount of Bitcoin,

47
00:02:32,518 --> 00:02:34,914
routed by best price after fees.

48
00:02:34,954 --> 00:02:35,751
Submit.

49
00:02:35,791 --> 00:02:38,238
Seven risk checks, a funding check,

50
00:02:38,278 --> 00:02:42,903
then the router walks every book cheapest
level first and sends a price-protected

51
00:02:42,943 --> 00:02:45,249
immediate-or-cancel child order.

52
00:02:45,289 --> 00:02:45,970
Filled.

53
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:51,679
And here is the proof: every exchange
ranked by all-in cost, price plus fee;

54
00:02:51,719 --> 00:02:52,877
the levels walked;

55
00:02:52,917 --> 00:02:55,120
the cap no child may cross;

56
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:56,988
expected versus actual;

57
00:02:57,028 --> 00:02:59,553
and what it saved against
the worst exchange.

58
00:02:59,593 --> 00:03:01,782
Every order keeps this proof.

59
00:03:02,774 --> 00:03:08,288
An order that breaks a rule: a fifth of a
Bitcoin, about sixteen thousand dollars,

60
00:03:08,328 --> 00:03:10,504
above this account's per-order limit.

61
00:03:10,544 --> 00:03:12,019
Submit.

62
00:03:12,059 --> 00:03:15,318
Refused by pre-trade risk,
with the check that failed,

63
00:03:15,358 --> 00:03:17,682
and nothing reached an exchange.

64
00:03:18,704 --> 00:03:21,241
A large order should not hit one exchange.

65
00:03:21,281 --> 00:03:26,100
The liquidity sweep sends to every exchange
that can fund it, at the same moment,

66
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:30,442
each part sized by that exchange's
depth, all under one price cap.

67
00:03:30,482 --> 00:03:31,963
A hundredth of a Bitcoin.

68
00:03:32,003 --> 00:03:33,454
Submit.

69
00:03:33,494 --> 00:03:38,389
The checks run, the children leave
together, each capped at the same price.

70
00:03:38,429 --> 00:03:40,644
One parent order, two exchanges,

71
00:03:40,684 --> 00:03:44,520
and the proof shows the split
and each exchange's fill.

72
00:03:45,705 --> 00:03:52,946
Large orders can be worked over time with
five execution algorithms: TWAP, VWAP,

73
00:03:52,986 --> 00:03:57,057
implementation shortfall,
percentage of volume, and iceberg.

74
00:03:57,097 --> 00:04:02,508
Or Auto reads the live books and this
order, picks one, and explains why;

75
00:04:02,548 --> 00:04:03,745
nothing is sent.

76
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:07,762
Every slice is pre-flight
checked against risk.

77
00:04:07,802 --> 00:04:11,770
Let's start a TWAP: six
slices over one minute,

78
00:04:11,810 --> 00:04:14,089
each one through the same order path.

79
00:04:14,129 --> 00:04:16,127
We'll come back for the result.

80
00:04:17,368 --> 00:04:20,060
Research runs on the
real market's candles:

81
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:24,428
five years of hourly and eight
years of daily history per coin.

82
00:04:24,468 --> 00:04:27,908
It forecasts the chance that
the next hour closes higher,

83
00:04:27,948 --> 00:04:32,508
with a likely range and its own track
record on data it had not seen —

84
00:04:32,548 --> 00:04:37,470
and mostly it says no proven edge, because
that is the honest answer on crypto data.

85
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:42,753
A backtester and a study of classic
techniques pay fees on every trade.

86
00:04:42,793 --> 00:04:46,587
Nothing here is advice, and
nothing here places an order.

87
00:04:47,899 --> 00:04:51,419
The copilot takes plain English.

88
00:04:51,459 --> 00:04:54,344
Buy twenty dollars of
SOL with smart routing.

89
00:04:54,384 --> 00:04:56,883
A language model only fills a form;

90
00:04:56,923 --> 00:05:02,437
the code validates every field, gathers
the live facts and builds the proposal.

91
00:05:02,477 --> 00:05:07,231
The model never holds keys,
never submits, never sizes risk.

92
00:05:07,271 --> 00:05:08,133
Here it is.

93
00:05:08,173 --> 00:05:09,524
It waits for a person.

94
00:05:09,564 --> 00:05:15,082
Confirm, and the order takes the same
path as the button, risk checks included.

95
00:05:15,928 --> 00:05:21,313
The TWAP is done: six slices,
each with its own fill,

96
00:05:21,353 --> 00:05:25,540
and the chart against the arrival price
and the real market's volume-weighted price

97
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:29,766
over the same minute, the two
benchmarks every algorithm reports.

98
00:05:29,806 --> 00:05:33,194
The Wallet reads real
balances from every exchange,

99
00:05:33,234 --> 00:05:36,313
keeps a P and L ledger from every fill,

100
00:05:36,353 --> 00:05:39,560
and reconciles it against
what each exchange reports.

101
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:42,937
A mismatch is shown, never edited away.

102
00:05:44,190 --> 00:05:45,000
Safety.

103
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:49,665
The kill switch stops every new
order, algorithm and bot at once.

104
00:05:49,705 --> 00:05:56,219
Engage it, with a reason.

105
00:05:56,259 --> 00:06:01,078
The order button locks, and the first of
the seven checks refuses anything else.

106
00:06:01,118 --> 00:06:03,127
The switch survives restarts,

107
00:06:03,167 --> 00:06:07,263
and is replicated by a three-node
RAFT cluster with signed messages:

108
00:06:07,303 --> 00:06:11,129
engaging works without a
majority, releasing needs one.

109
00:06:11,169 --> 00:06:13,304
Let's kill the leader process.

110
00:06:13,344 --> 00:06:16,384
A new leader within seconds, nothing lost.

111
00:06:16,424 --> 00:06:20,933
Two of three is a majority, so
we release, and trading resumes.

112
00:06:20,973 --> 00:06:24,838
The limits, a loss limit that
engages the switch by itself,

113
00:06:24,878 --> 00:06:28,292
and concentration alerts
live on the Risk Manager.

114
00:06:28,771 --> 00:06:32,996
Also in the box: bots that trade
from a fenced automation fund,

115
00:06:33,036 --> 00:06:36,527
with a practice lab that measures
every one of their trades;

116
00:06:36,567 --> 00:06:41,940
an arbitrage detector that watches every
exchange pair after fees and, by design,

117
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:43,164
cannot trade;

118
00:06:43,204 --> 00:06:44,285
venue health;

119
00:06:44,325 --> 00:06:49,038
and the audit log, the append-only
record of every step with who did it.

120
00:06:50,503 --> 00:06:55,063
Everything so far ran in the
exchanges' sandboxes, with play money.

121
00:06:55,103 --> 00:06:59,110
The same code runs on a separate
real-money server with a fixed IP,

122
00:06:59,150 --> 00:07:01,674
reached only through an SSH tunnel.

123
00:07:01,714 --> 00:07:06,146
An exchange gets orders only after it
confirms that our key can trade but cannot

124
00:07:06,186 --> 00:07:07,087
withdraw.

125
00:07:07,127 --> 00:07:11,107
Hard caps live in the server's settings
where no screen can loosen them,

126
00:07:11,147 --> 00:07:15,205
going live is typed, alerts
reach a private Discord channel,

127
00:07:15,245 --> 00:07:18,246
and a stop from the phone
engages the kill switch;

128
00:07:18,286 --> 00:07:20,514
nothing releases it from the phone.

129
00:07:21,515 --> 00:07:26,398
The contribution is the integration:
real exchanges, fee-aware routing,

130
00:07:26,438 --> 00:07:31,926
deterministic risk, algorithms,
research, a copilot a person confirms,

131
00:07:31,966 --> 00:07:35,457
and an audit trail, one
system, verified live.

132
00:07:35,497 --> 00:07:40,432
ApnaSOR, group CS14, Usman
Institute of Technology,

133
00:07:40,472 --> 00:07:43,149
supervised by Sir Shamaail Jafri.
